# Deployment template. Copy to `.env.deploy` on the server and edit.
#
#     cp .env.deploy.example .env.deploy
#
# `.env.deploy` is untracked and must stay that way; `.gitignore` refuses it.
# This file -- the template -- is tracked, so nothing in it may ever become a
# secret.
#
# WHAT MAY LIVE HERE
#
# Operational facts about one machine: which hostname it answers to, which host
# ports the proxy publishes, which routing policy is in force, how loudly to log.
#
# WHAT MAY NOT, EVER
#
# * A password, a token, an API key, a private key, or a cloud credential. This
#   system accepts no credential material anywhere -- not from a request, not
#   from the dashboard, not from configuration -- so a secret here would be one
#   with nowhere to go and a file to leak out of.
#
# * Anything scientific. Not a model identifier, not a champion, not a decision
#   threshold, not a fusion strategy, not a calibrator, not a feature allowlist.
#   Those were chosen on validation evidence and frozen, and a deployment that
#   could restate one from an environment file would be publishing a different
#   system under the same name without leaving a record. `APISettings` and
#   `DashboardSettings` decline to declare such a field at all, and the
#   deployment tests assert that no variable named in the merged Compose
#   configuration could reach one.
#
# Compose reads this file automatically when it is named `.env`, and NOT when it
# is named `.env.deploy`. Pass it explicitly:
#
#     docker compose --env-file .env.deploy \
#       -f compose.yaml -f compose.deploy.yaml up -d --build
#
# Every variable below has a working default in `compose.deploy.yaml`, so a
# deployment with no `.env.deploy` at all comes up in IP-only mode on port 80.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mode 1 -- IP-only smoke test (the default)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plain HTTP on the droplet's address. No DNS record needed, no certificate
# issued, nothing to get wrong before you have seen the thing work once.
#
# Browse to http://<droplet-ip>/
PAD_SITE_ADDRESS=:80

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mode 2 -- hostname + HTTPS (the final demonstration)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Point an A record at the droplet first, wait for it to resolve, then set the
# hostname here and restart. Caddy obtains and renews the certificate itself
# over ACME; no certificate and no private key is stored in this repository, and
# none may be committed.
#
# Substitute your own name. `example.org` is the RFC 2606 reserved name and is
# not a real deployment -- this project has none.
#
# PAD_SITE_ADDRESS=demo.example.org

# HSTS. Only meaningful once mode 2 is actually serving TLS.
#
# The default `max-age=0` is deliberate rather than absent: it instructs a
# browser to forget any pin it holds for this origin, which is the honest
# instruction from an HTTP-only deployment. Turn it on only after HTTPS works,
# and understand that it is hard to undo -- a browser that has seen a long
# max-age will refuse plain HTTP to this hostname until it expires.
PAD_HSTS=max-age=0
# PAD_HSTS=max-age=31536000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public routing policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which reviewed Caddyfile is in force. Both are tracked files under
# deploy/caddy/ and neither accepts an upstream from the environment -- this
# variable selects between two audited policies, it does not compose a new one.
#
#   ./deploy/caddy/Caddyfile           the console only. The API is unreachable
#                                      from the internet; the dashboard still
#                                      reaches it over the Compose network, so
#                                      detection and replay work in full.
#
#   ./deploy/caddy/Caddyfile.api-docs  additionally publishes Swagger, the
#                                      OpenAPI schema, /health, /ready,
#                                      /version, the system and model reports,
#                                      the rule catalog, and the replay scenario
#                                      catalog. Read-only routes only: the
#                                      scoring and replay-control endpoints stay
#                                      internal in both policies.
PAD_PROXY_CADDYFILE=./deploy/caddy/Caddyfile

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the proxy publishes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A whole publish specification, not a bare port, so it can also carry a bind
# address. `80` and `443` publish on every interface, which is what a public
# demonstration wants. `127.0.0.1:8080` is what the local topology test uses to
# exercise this exact configuration without taking a privileged port.
#
# These are the ONLY ports this deployment publishes. The API's 8000 and the
# console's 8501 are not published at all -- `compose.deploy.yaml` removes them
# -- and nothing here can bring them back.
PAD_PROXY_HTTP_PUBLISH=80
PAD_PROXY_HTTPS_PUBLISH=443

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not configured here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API's own environment -- artifact root, allowlist path, feature/ML/rule
# configuration paths, replay switches, log level -- is declared in
# `compose.yaml` and is not parameterised by this file. That is on purpose: a
# deployment file that could repoint the artifact root could point a running
# service at a bundle nobody reviewed, and the point of the serving-bundle
# contract is that it cannot.
#
# To change the API's log verbosity, edit PAD_API_LOG_LEVEL in `compose.yaml`
# and redeploy. It is a reviewed change to a tracked file, which is what a
# change to what a deployment records should be.
